### 15. Server-Side Socket.io Integration (`server/server.js` & `server/src/index.ts`)

```javascript
import http from 'http';
import express from 'express';
import { Server } from 'socket.io';

const app = express();
const httpServer = http.createServer(app);

// Attach Socket.io with CORS restricted strictly to CLIENT_URL
const io = new Server(httpServer, {
  cors: {
    origin: process.env.CLIENT_URL || 'http://localhost:5173',
    methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PATCH', 'DELETE'],
    credentials: true
  }
});

// Log real-time connection lifecycle
io.on('connection', (socket) => {
  console.log(`⚡ [Socket.io] Client connected: ${socket.id}`);

  socket.on('disconnect', (reason) => {
    console.log(`🔌 [Socket.io] Client disconnected: ${socket.id} (${reason})`);
  });
});

// Store io on Express app so any controller can access it:
// const io = req.app.get('io');
// io.emit('booking:updated', updatedBooking);
app.set('io', io);

httpServer.listen(PORT);
```

- **What it does:**
  1. Wraps the Express application within a Node `http.Server` instance.
  2. Instantiates `socket.io.Server`, applying CORS rules so only requests from `CLIENT_URL` (typically `http://localhost:5173`) can establish WebSocket channels.
  3. Binds connection/disconnection handlers to log real-time diagnostics.
  4. Stores the active `io` instance directly on the Express app via `app.set('io', io)`.
- **Why required by system:**
  - Standard Express route handlers run in isolation. By binding `io` to the app dictionary, any controller across any route file can retrieve the broadcaster via `req.app.get('io')` without importing a singleton or creating circular dependencies.
  - CORS restriction protects your WebSocket gateway from unauthorized cross-origin socket flooding.

